sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch07-20> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "There is no direct correspondence between the complexity of a system's underlying rules and the complexity of its behavior: some of the simplest rules yield highly complex behavior, fairly complicated rules often yield simple behavior, and rules differing at a single position can behave very differently.";
    dc:type "computational-observation";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Rule complexity does not determine behavioral complexity";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 7, “Mechanisms in Programs and Nature”; section “Origins of Simple Behavior” (from p. 351), p. 351. Scope: simple programs such as cellular automata, and by extension natural systems"
}
